From: Francis Lachance <lachance () dmcs ! dnd ! ca>
Date: Wed, 31 Jan 2001 13:46:48 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: SUMMARY: can't log in as root since mkpasswd
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=98094920303731

Thanks to Pat, Mario, Jim, Bac and IvanHoe for their responses.

Suggestions were to go to single user mode to change the password, boot 
with the cd and change the password, try to su from another account that 
has system privileges, restore passwd file.

I should have put in more details about the problem but here is what happened.

I changed my account password on a 4.0D system. After changing it, I was 
prompted to run the mkpasswd /etc/passwd command which I did.
The next morning when I tried to log in it wouldn't let me, I thought I 
must have typed my new password wrong when I set it up the day before. I'll 
just go log in as root at the console and change it later on.
So I go to the console try log in as root and it won't let me. Ouch !
So I get the password of the other person who has system privileges and log 
in and it works but I can't su to root.
I take a look at the passwd file, root is there but my account is gone, I 
also notice two old accounts that I had removed some time ago that are back 
and another account that I know should be there is gone. Maybe  and old 
passwd file has somehow been restored ? No because there are also recent 
accounts in the passwd file. What the ...

So I look at the date of my passwd file and its from beginning of December, 
hmm that's wrong, the date of passwd.dir and passwd.pag are Jan 29 the day 
I changed my password and ran the mkpasswd command.
Anyway since some old accounts are back I track the previous system admin 
of the box and ask him if he remembers the password from back then. I try 
the old root password and I'm in.
I also restored the passwd file (in a different directory) from the day 
before I changed my password. And that passwd file was also wrong i.e. my 
account information wasn't there.
So today I will be digging into passwd, passwd.dir and passwd.pag. I'll try 
to find what could have happen and how exactly those files affect each other.

So to resume, don't blindly do what the systems tells you to do ( I knew 
that but I guess I was running in dumb mode that day).

Francis 

