-----------</SPAN></SPAN></SPAN></SPAN></FONT></FONT></SPAN></SPAN></SPA=
N></SPAN></SPAN></FONT></FONT><SPAN=20
  class=3D866205112-09012001><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>Good =
Day,</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>The panic "simple_lock: time =
limit=20
  exceeded" indicates one of the cpus</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>tried to acquire a spin lock =
that was=20
  being held by another cpu. The one</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>that is trying to acquire the =
spinlock=20
  will wait 15 seconds before it</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>determines there is a fatal =
problem and=20
  calls panic() to crash the</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>system. </FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>Typically, this requires an =
in-depth=20
  analysis of the crash files to</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>isolate the problem; however, =
you may get=20
  an idea as to what area of</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>code was involved by looking =
at the=20
  crash-data file in /var/adm/crash</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>directory. In this directory =
you will find=20
  three files that together</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>form the set of "crash dump =
files". The=20
  three files in each set have the</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>same number appended to them. =
In the=20
  crash-data file, you will find a</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>stack trace that will reveal =
what function=20
  calls were being made that</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>resulted in this crash. If =
you edit that=20
  file, search for the string </FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>"tset=20
  machine_slot[paniccpu].cpu_panic_thread:"</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>Following that, you will see =
the stack=20
  trace; If you post that trace, it</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>may reveal a known issue. If =
it is a=20
  readily identifiable issue, we can</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>direct you... if not, a crash =
analysis=20
  must be done by Compaq Unix</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>Support.</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>I hope this is =
helpful...</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>Regards,</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>Whitney Latta<SPAN=20
  class=3D866205112-09012001></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff><FONT face=3DVerdana>&nbsp;<SPAN=20
  class=3D866205112-09012001><SPAN class=3D866205112-09012001><SPAN=20
  class=3D866205112-09012001><SPAN=20
  =
class=3D866205112-09012001>---------------------------------------------=
------------------------------------------------------------------------=
-----------</SPAN></SPAN></SPAN></SPAN></FONT></FONT></SPAN></FONT><FONT=
=20
  color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>If you really want to know what made =
the system=20
  panic, you need to</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>get into the /var/adm/crash =
directory and=20
  look at the crash-data</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>file that was created during =
the=20
  reboot.</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>All that shows up in your =
"UERF" output is=20
  that you had a simple</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>lock timeout failure. The =
output you=20
  posted and the rest of your</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>problem description doesn't =
even indicate=20
  what system model you</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>have or what version of =
software you are=20
  running, so no one can</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>say much more with any=20
  authority.</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>For what it's worth, a simple =
lock timeout=20
  is USUALLY a software</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>problem, but it can be caused =
by=20
  mis-behaving hardware. There are</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>lots of data structures =
inside the kernel=20
  that are accessible from</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>different CPUs in a =
multi-processor=20
  system, so there are locking</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>mechanisms to coordinate =
access. If one of=20
  the CPUs can't get the</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>simple lock (a "spin lock") =
for a=20
  particular data structure in a</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>reasonable amount of time (it =
should never=20
  take really long), then</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>it panic-s the system. That's =
what=20
  happened here. But you can't</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>tell WHY it happened -- that =
is, where the=20
  kernel was running when</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>the problem occurred -- =
because that=20
  information isn't recorded in</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>your binary error log file =
(so UERF can't=20
  report it).</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff><FONT face=3DVerdana>Tom<SPAN=20
  class=3D866205112-09012001>&nbsp;</SPAN></FONT></FONT></P><SPAN=20
  class=3D866205112-09012001>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff><FONT face=3DVerdana>&nbsp;<SPAN=20
  class=3D866205112-09012001><SPAN class=3D866205112-09012001><SPAN=20
  class=3D866205112-09012001><SPAN=20
  =
class=3D866205112-09012001>---------------------------------------------=
------------------------------------------------------------------------=
-----------</SPAN></SPAN></SPAN></SPAN></FONT></FONT></P>
  <P><SPAN class=3D866205112-09012001><SPAN=20
  class=3D866205112-09012001></SPAN></SPAN></SPAN><SPAN=20
  class=3D866205112-09012001><FONT color=3D#0000ff =
face=3DVerdana>Panics are software=20
  failures. This one is particular to</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>SMP lock handling. Check the =
Services web=20
  site for the</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>patch kits for your =
particular version and=20
  see if they</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>have a patch for the symtom. =
If not, call=20
  your country</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>support center and report the =
problem. If=20
  you don't have</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>a software support contract, =
they may=20
  charge per-call to</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff face=3DVerdana>handle it.</FONT></P>
  <P><FONT color=3D#0000ff=20
face=3DVerdana>&nbsp;</FONT></SPAN></P></SPAN><BR></BLOCKQUOTE></BODY></=
